Recorded while landing karr #92 / ADR 0019, which warns about this rather than
fixing it.

A `True` STRING in an unencrypted YAML slot is written bare, because libyaml
does not quote a spelling its own resolver does not know. Go reads a boolean
out of it. The divergence disappears entirely if the token is QUOTED, and the
measurement says that is what sops itself writes for the same value:

  plaintext leaf         sops -e writes            we read it back
  x_unencrypted: True    x_unencrypted: true       bool
  x_unencrypted: "True"  x_unencrypted: "True"     str

So `"True"` is not a retyping - it is the document sops produces for the string
the caller has, the MAC is unchanged (the digest text is `True` either way),
and both implementations then read a string. ADR 0013 rejected "quote the leaf
on the way out" for `mode: 0755`, where quoting turns an INT into a string;
that reason does not apply to a leaf that is already a string.

Two things stop it from being ADR 0019 decision:

  - It moves wire bytes for documents that are accepted today. That is a change
    to what this distribution writes, not a diagnostic, and wants its own ADR
    and its own corpus.
  - YAML::XS has no per-scalar style control. No tag, no forced-quote hook, and
    the dualvar carrier ADR 0011 uses writes its PV as a PLAIN scalar (measured:
    dualvar(0.3, "hello") emits `v: hello`). The only route is text surgery on
    the finished document, which _quote_sops_timestamp can do safely only
    because it targets one known key inside one known block. For an arbitrary
    key path at arbitrary nesting that is a new mechanism whose failure mode -
    a corrupted document - is worse than the divergence it fixes.

Anything here would have to prove itself against a corpus the size of ADR 0019
(91 leaves x 2 slots x both handlers) and show that no other leaf class gets
quoted by accident. Wire lane. Measured with sops 3.13.3.
MEASURED FROM karr #135 (docs/adr/0039): this ticket is now the gate on a second,
larger class, and the measurement says what "a real emitter" has to buy.

The same missing capability -- one quoted scalar -- blocks 22 measured spellings
that are a STRING on both sides, that sops writes double-quoted and reads back at
exit 0, and that File::SOPS refuses to produce (ADR 0038's corpus; a 23rd row,
"0xffffffffffffffff", found in #135). That is a refusal of documents sops writes,
not a divergence like this ticket's own True/False row.

Three routes were probed and all three are dead, so the emitter really is the
gate: (a) $YAML::XS::QuoteNumericStrings is a boolean and quotes exactly what
Perl's looks_like_number accepts, at every setting; (b) NO SV shape makes
YAML::XS quote a given text -- and a dualvar carrier makes it WORSE, writing even
'0755' bare, because the emitter's rule reads the SV; (c) reject_scalar cannot
replace a leaf (its return value is discarded), so surgery needs a second full
tree walk in emit.

WITH THIS TICKET ALONE, 7 of the 22 become writable (the non-finite tokens --
the only family where a bare and a quoted source do not arrive as the same Perl
string, thanks to ADR 0026/0034). The other 15 need karr #127 as well.
